// The Theban alphabet.
//
// From the wing's research file theban.verified.json. The signs are traced
// from the first printing, Trithemius's Polygraphia of July 1518 (Book VI,
// sig. 2q5v), in the Wellcome Collection copy, and checked sign by sign against
// the Library of Congress copy of the same setting; Agrippa's re-cut table of
// 1533 (III.29; McGill, Library of Congress and Jung copies), the English of
// 1651, the French of 1561 and the Latin of 1613 were compared
// (traced/theban/glyphs.json, independently checked). Each glyph is the traced
// path data unchanged, so the page draws it exactly as the tracer's specimen
// sheet does; the four signs Agrippa re-cut most are shown beside them as he
// printed them. A letter's note carries only what the tracing tells a reader
// about that letter. The plate is the same page of the Wellcome copy, which
// Wellcome Collection marks with the Public Domain Mark.
