        follows: 'Trithemius, Polygraphiae libri sex ([Basel or Oppenheim]: at the cost of Johannes Haselberg, July 1518), Book VI, sig. 2q5v (the printing is unpaginated), the table at the head of the page, introduced at the foot of 2q5r by “Sequitur aliud alphabetum Honorij cognomento Thebani”: three rows of eight black signs, each with its letter printed in red above it; in the Wellcome Collection copy, London, EPB/D/6367.2 (copy 1), digitised by the Wellcome Library, Internet Archive b33552137, image 0506.',
        drawn: 'Each sign is traced from that page as the centre line of its printed strokes, all twenty-four at one scale, so that they keep their printed sizes, and set on the printed top line of their row. The drawing keeps what the print shows: every stroke, flag and foot of the blackletter pieces the signs are built from, which counters are closed and which open, and the parts that stand apart, such as the small n of I and the foot of R, which stand alike in both copies and so belong to what was printed, not to damage. The rest is the site’s convention: one even round pen, where the printed strokes are heavy and their ends square, its caps trimmed back to the printed stroke ends; red labels printed out of register against the signs are left out, and only the black is drawn. Where the Wellcome copy is blotted, as in the lower part of the final sign, or the two copies differ in a hairline, the letter’s note says which copy is followed.',
        variants: 'Compared sign by sign with the Library of Congress copy of 1518 (Rare Book and Special Collections Division, Z103 .T83 1518, copy 2), the same setting: the same signs and labels, with a few hairline joins open where the Wellcome copy prints them closed. Agrippa’s table in De occulta philosophia III.29 (July 1533, p. CCLXXIII, sig. z3r; the McGill, Library of Congress and Jung copies, which print the same cuts) re-cut every sign smoothly in two rows of twelve, with roman capitals below; most keep the plan of 1518, but I loses its small n, R becomes one sign with no detached foot, V loses the small u in its crook, and the final sign gains a small ω and the label Ω: those four are drawn beside the letters from the McGill copy. The English of 1651 (Wellcome copy and a second copy at the Internet Archive, p. 438) follows 1533; the French of 1561 (Boston Public Library copy, fol. 184v) and the Latin of 1613 (p. 597) keep the plan of 1518, the French labelling the final sign &. The re-cut tables, Barrett’s plate of 1801, retouched modern crops and modern fonts are not used as a base.',
