            glyph: { d: 'M 30.2 21.8 L 33 23.3 L 33.3 31 L 41.4 28.5 M 33.3 31 L 30.5 34 M 33 23.4 L 37 22.7 L 41.8 26 M 52.9 20.5 L 44 23.3 L 41.6 28.5 L 39.6 40.5 L 38.3 53.5 L 34.5 61.5 M 39.6 40.5 L 33 42.2 L 29.4 48 M 30.8 41 L 33 42.2 M 38.4 53.5 Q 42 50.5 45.3 52 L 46.9 59 M 45.4 52 Q 49.5 48.5 54.2 50.7 L 54 54 L 58.6 58.6 M 54.2 50.7 Q 58.5 48.5 63.3 52.2 L 63.2 57 Q 62.6 60.2 59.6 64.4 L 51.4 76.6 M 63.6 54 Q 67.4 60 64.4 66 L 56.2 78.2 M 63.5 51.8 L 69.4 49.6 L 70.6 46.4 M 43.8 82 L 52.8 77 L 56.5 80.8' },
            strokes: 'A head of a short vertical closed into the stem at top and foot, with a flag at the left and a long flag to the top right; a bar runs left from the stem and forks at its end, down and up; at the stem’s foot a textura m opens to the right, ending in a flag; from its last legs a broad descender runs down to the left onto a base.',
            note: 'Labelled w in red in both copies of 1518, and w again in the Latin of 1613. On the page before (sig. 2q5r), of the cipher printed there, Trithemius says that Latin has no need of k, y and w but that he has brought the alphabet to twenty-four places because k and w are used in German; the Theban table, too, has twenty-four places. Agrippa (1533) sets a small ω at its foot and labels it Ω, the English of 1651 marks it with a small omega-like sign, and the French of 1561 labels it &; Barrett and later copyists took it for an ampersand or an end-mark. A Wikipedia talk-page editor’s theory — that a W standing out of order was misread as ω — is one editor’s suggestion, not scholarship. In the Wellcome copy the descender and the right of the m are a blot: they are drawn from the Library of Congress copy, which agrees where both are clear, and the broad descender is drawn with two passes of the pen. The transliterator never writes it.',
